{
  "an1.333:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 1.333–377 ",
  "an1.333:0.2": "Chapter Four ",
  "an1.333:1.0": "333 ",
  "an1.333:1.1": "“Just as, mendicants, in the Black Plum Tree Land the delightful parks, woods, meadows, and lotus ponds are few, ",
  "an1.333:1.2": "while the hilly terrain, inaccessible riverlands, stumps and thorns, and rugged mountains are many; ",
  "an1.333:1.3": "so too the sentient beings born on land are few, while those born in water are many.” ",
  "an1.334:1.0": "334 ",
  "an1.334:1.1": "“… so too the sentient beings reborn as humans are few, ",
  "an1.334:1.2": "while those not reborn as humans are many.” ",
  "an1.335:1.0": "335 ",
  "an1.335:1.1": "“… so too the sentient beings reborn in the central countries are few, ",
  "an1.335:1.2": "while those reborn in the borderlands, among uneducated foreigners, are many.” ",
  "an1.336:1.0": "336 ",
  "an1.336:1.1": "“… so too the sentient beings who are wise, bright, clever, and able to distinguish what is well said from what is poorly said are few, ",
  "an1.336:1.2": "while the sentient beings who are witless, dull, idiotic, and unable to distinguish what is well said from what is poorly said are many.” ",
  "an1.337:1.0": "337 ",
  "an1.337:1.1": "“… so too the sentient beings who have the noble eye of wisdom are few, ",
  "an1.337:1.2": "while those who are ignorant and confused are many.” ",
  "an1.338:1.0": "338 ",
  "an1.338:1.1": "“… so too the sentient beings who get to see a Realized One are few,